## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to unpack bit-packed fields without branches—a common low-level task in networking, codecs, and embedded systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Bitwise masks and shifts** extract subfields from packed integers efficiently
- **No branches** ensures constant-time execution, critical for performance-sensitive code
- **Endianness** is irrelevant when masking at exact bit positions

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t  version;
    uint8_t  type;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t  flags;
} Header;

Header extractHeaderFields(uint32_t w) {
    Header h;
    h.version = (w >> 28) & 0xF;  // Bits 31-28
    h.type    = (w >> 24) & 0xF;  // Bits 27-24
    h.length  = (w >> 8)  & 0xFFFF; // Bits 23-8
    h.flags   = w & 0xFF;          // Bits 7-0
    return h;
}
```

Each shift moves the target field to the least significant position, then the mask isolates the relevant bits. This approach is branchless, deterministic, and executes in constant time.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary bit interactions and implement constant-time checks using only bitwise ops. 

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise shifts and masks across multiword values
- Detecting adjacent bits within each 64-bit half and across the 64-bit boundary
- Branchless boolean results for interview clarity

## Code Example
```javascript
int hasAdjacentOnes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo & (x.lo << 1);
  uint64_t b = x.hi & (x.hi << 1);
  int cross = ((x.lo >> 63) & 1) & (x.hi & 1);
  return (a | b | cross) != 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to detect a run of exactly k adjacent ones? 
- How would you implement a similar check for 256-bit values represented as two uint128s?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft branchless 128-bit comparison, a common low-level operation in high-performance contexts.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless selection using masks
- 128-bit comparison via hi/lo parts
- Handling -1/0/1 outputs reliably

## Code Example

```c
int compare128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  int hi = (int)(a.hi > b.hi) - (int)(a.hi < b.hi);
  int lo = (int)(a.lo > b.lo) - (int)(a.lo < b.lo);
  int t  = (hi != 0);
  int m  = -t;
  return (hi & m) | (lo & ~m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to unsigned 128-bit values?
- How would you extend to return an enum instead of int?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
