## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform full-width bit reversal in constant time using SWAR patterns, not per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit composition as hi/lo 64-bit
- SWAR bit-reversal: 1,2,4,8,16,32-bit block swaps
- Final cross-half swap to complete reversal

## Code Example
```javascript
// 64-bit reversal helper
static uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to larger widths (256-bit) with minimal extra code?
- What intrinsics or compiler options help optimize this on typical CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked

- Checks 128-bit handling and branchless bit counting, a common crypto/DB pattern.
- Evaluates ability to compose primitives without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Splitting 128-bit into hi/lo and aggregating results
- Constant-time, no branches

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to decompose a 128‑bit value into four 32‑bit lanes and re‑assemble them without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Lane extraction via masks and shifts
- 128‑bit as hi/lo pairs with 32‑bit lanes
- Branchless permutation and safe packing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 permute32x4_128(uint128 x) {
  uint32 A = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32 B = (uint32)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint32 C = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32 D = (uint32)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint128 y;
  y.hi = ((uint64_t)B << 32) | A;
  y.lo = ((uint64_t)D << 32) | C;
  return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to permute eight 16‑bit lanes within 128‑bit? 
- What tests validate boundary conditions (e.g., when A/B/C/D are all zeros or all ones)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to decompose a 128-bit operation into smaller parallel units and efficiently apply a well-known branchless counting technique.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount for 32-bit lanes
- 128-bit value decomposition into hi/lo components
- Branchless bit manipulation and final reduction

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount32(unsigned int x) {
    x = x - ((x>>1)&0x55555555);
    x = (x&0x33333333)+((x>>2)&0x33333333);
    x = (x+(x>>4))&0x0F0F0F0F;
    return (x*0x01010101)>>24;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount32(x.lo) + popcount32(x.lo >> 32) + 
           popcount32(x.hi) + popcount32(x.hi >> 32);
}
```

## Quick Tests
```c
assert(popcount128(0) == 0);
assert(popcount128(UINT128_MAX) == 128);
assert(popcount128(0xF0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0) == 32);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits to form Morton codes is common in spatial indexing and GPU data layouts. The solution enforces branchless bit spreading using fixed masks, ensuring deterministic performance.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order (Z-order) bit interleaving
- Fixed-mask bit spreading (part1by1)
- Branchless implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint32_t n) {
  uint64_t v = n;
  v = (v | (v << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  v = (v | (v << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  v = (v | (v << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return v;
}

uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs producing a 128-bit result?
- How does this interact with SIMD paths for batched data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design constant-time bit-manipulation without branches for 128-bit values, a common low-latency constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-reversal techniques
- 128-bit word handling
- Branchless bit tricks

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x=(x>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL | (x&0x5555555555555555ULL)<<1;
  x=(x>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL | (x&0x3333333333333333ULL)<<2;
  x=(x>>4)&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL | (x&0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL)<<4;
  x=(x>>8)&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL | (x&0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL)<<8;
  x=(x>>16)&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL | (x&0x0000ffff0000ffffULL)<<16;
  x=(x>>32)&0x00000000ffffffffULL | (x&0x00000000ffffffffULL)<<32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.lo); r.hi = reverse64(x.hi); uint64_t t=r.lo; r.lo=r.hi; r.hi=t; return r; }
unsigned isBitPalindrome128(uint128 x){ uint128 r = reverse128(x); return (x.hi==r.hi && x.lo==r.lo) ? 1 : 0; }
```

